<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.5.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02403</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.5.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e42e4195-132d-4fe9-a971-f8933acd16ed</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Solve each equation and write the solutions using the ± notation, then choose the correct solution from the list.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(x^2=144\)</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(x= \pm\; 144\) </item>
  <item> \(x= \pm\; 72\) </item>
  <item> \(x= \pm\; 12\) </item>
  <item> \(x= \pm\; 24\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis>
  \(x= \pm\; 12\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(x^2=5\)</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(x= \pm\; \sqrt{5}\) </item>
  <item> \(x= \pm\; 5\) </item>
  <item> \(x= \pm\; \frac{5}{2}\)   </item>
  <item>\(
    x= \pm\; \sqrt{10}\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x= \pm\; \sqrt{5}\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(4x^2=28\)</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(x= \pm\; \sqrt {\frac{7}{2}}\) </item>
  <item>\( x= \pm\; 7\) </item>
  <item> \(x= \pm\; \frac{7}{2}\)   </item>
  <item> \(x= \pm\; \sqrt{7}\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x= \pm\; \sqrt{7}\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(x^2=\frac{25}{4}\)</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(x= \pm\; \frac{25}{4}\)</item>
  <item> \(x= \pm\; \frac{5}{2}\) </item>
  <item> \(x= \pm\; \frac{10}{2}\) </item>
  <item> \(x= \pm\; \frac{5}{4}\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis>
  \(x= \pm\; \frac{5}{2}\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(2x^2=22\)</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(x= \pm\; \sqrt{\frac{11}{2}}\) </item>
  <item> \(x= \pm\; 11\) </item>
  <item> \(x= \pm\; \frac{11}{2}\)   </item>
  <item> \(x= \pm\; \sqrt{11}\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis>
  \(x= \pm\;\sqrt{11}\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(7x^2=16\)</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(x= \pm\; \sqrt{\frac{16}{7}}\) </item>
  <item> \(x= \pm\; 16\) </item>
  <item> \(x= \pm\; 8\)   </item>
  <item> \(x= \pm \;\frac {\sqrt{16}}{7}\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x= \pm \;\sqrt{\frac{16}{7}}\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>For each expression, choose an equivalent expression.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(10 \pm\sqrt{4}\)</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> -17 and 5 </item>
  <item> \(4+ \sqrt{2}\) and \(4- \sqrt{2}\) </item>
  <item> 8 and 12 </item>
  <item> 3 and 5 </item>
  <item> \(4+ \sqrt{10}\) and \(4- \sqrt{10}\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 8 and 12</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(4 \pm\sqrt{10}\)</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> -17 and 5 </item>
  <item> \(4+ \sqrt{2}\) and \(4- \sqrt{2}\) </item>
  <item> 8 and 12 </item>
  <item> 3 and 5 </item>
  <item> \(4+ \sqrt{10}\) and \(4- \sqrt{10}\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis>
  \(4+ \sqrt{10}\) and \(4- \sqrt{10}\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(\sqrt{16} \pm\sqrt{2}\)</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> -17 and 5 </item>
  <item>\(4+ \sqrt{2}\) and \(4- \sqrt{2}\) </item>
  <item> 8 and 12 </item>
  <item> 3 and 5 </item>
  <item> \(4+ \sqrt{10}\) and \(4- \sqrt{10}\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(4+ \sqrt{2}\) and \(4- \sqrt{2}\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the difference between \(\sqrt{9}\) and the solutions to \(x^2=9\)?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(\sqrt{9}\) is equal to 3, while \(x^2=9\) is equal to -3. </item>
  <item> \(\sqrt{9}\) is equal to -3, while there are two numbers that make \(x^2=9\) true: 3 and -3. </item>
  <item> \(\sqrt{9}\) is equal to 3, while there are two numbers that make \(x^2=9\) true: 3 and -3. </item>
  <item> There is no difference; they are equal.  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis>
  \(\sqrt{9}\) is equal to 3, while there are two numbers that make \(x^2=9\) true: 3 and -3.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Technology required. For each equation, find the exact solutions by completing the square and the approximate solutions by graphing. Then, verify that the solutions found using the two methods are close.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> Solve the equation \(x^2+10x+8=0\) by completing the square. <emphasis>Select two</emphasis> exact solutions.</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(x= -5+ \sqrt{17}\) </item>
  <item>\(x=5+ \sqrt{17}\) </item>
  <item> \(x=5- \sqrt{17}\) </item>
  <item> \(x= -5- \sqrt{17}\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis>
  \(x= -5+ \sqrt{17}\), \(x= -5- \sqrt{17}\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Choose the graph that best represents the equation \(x^2+10x+8=0\).</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9406eab7a0c9ca8be81ab3b1231099c82f6740b8"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ce2e1c819f40a1056015664904a30aa436866e09"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/2ef2964dd6a6f0f40ff2b04d05a30c9db470be0d"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c49ec8e6ffdb34fd50c28da8cff08ace3d38cab2"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c49ec8e6ffdb34fd50c28da8cff08ace3d38cab2"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Are the solutions and the graph for the equation \(x^2+10x+8=0\) similar? </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> They are not similar.  </item>
  <item> They are similar because the solutions are close.  </item>
  <item> They are not similar because the square root of a number is always positive. </item>
  <item> They are similar because the square root of a number is always positive. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> They are similar because the solutions are close. </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Answer the following questions about \(x^2-4x-11=0\).</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Solve the equation \(x^2-4x-11=0\) by completing the square. <emphasis>Select two</emphasis> exact solutions.</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(x=-2+ \sqrt{15}\) </item>
  <item> \(x=2+ \sqrt{15}\) </item>
  <item> \(x=2- \sqrt{15}\) </item>
  <item> \(x=-2- \sqrt{15}\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x=2+ \sqrt{15}\), \(x=2-\sqrt{15}\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Choose the graph that best represents the equation \(x^2-4x-11=0\).    </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item type="A"> <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/041e8dc43448e170283ce1fa6d615adfe3dac8e4"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item type="A"> <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/e93edafd840fe7d3ab7494edb47bcf276250779b"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/03435b7e473da5a2f72742a8f6b0c0a119cb9379"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/dcd702a64a9589ebd4139bc950b0487352e9b6d4"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/041e8dc43448e170283ce1fa6d615adfe3dac8e4"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Are the solutions and the graph for the equation \(x^2-4x-11=0\) similar? </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> They are similar because the solutions are close.  </item>
  <item> They are not similar because the square root of a number is always positive. </item>
  <item> They are not similar.  </item>
  <item> They are similar because the square root of a number is always positive. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> They are similar because the solutions are close. </para></section>
